#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-13411" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6$?\n\n1. $(a-2, b-6)$\n2. $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n3. $(a+2, b-6)$\n4. $(a-2,b+ 6)$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4$.\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4$. After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(a+2,b)$.\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6$. After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(a+2,b-6)$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n$b=a^4$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute\n\n$$\nx = a + 2\n$$\n\ninto this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (a + 2 - 2)^{4} - 6 \\\\\n&= a^{4} - 6 \\\\\n&= b - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when\n\n$$\nx = a + 2\n$$\n\n, we get $y = b - 6$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-13412" :question/problem "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1$. What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1$.\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x)$. After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,0+1)=(3,1)$.\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x)$. After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,-(1)) = (3,-1)$.\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3$. After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,-1+3)=(3,2)$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f(3)=1\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -f(3) + 3 \\\\\n&=-1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059359 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a0969b02-52c8-5d22-b3e0-b594d9fbd4d8" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-13413" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x)$. What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/141/Source/Images/f409140a46a8034453948c18db9672e9.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$. So, we need to find the image of $(2,6)$, the local maximum of $y=f(x)$, under the transformation.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = f(2x)-1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,6)$.\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x)$. After this transformation, the point $(2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n$\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 2, 6 \\right) = (1,6)$.\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit downward and get the graph of $y = f(2x)-1$. After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(1,6-1) = (1,5)$.\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$ is $(1,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcf626c8-d2f0-51ca-9639-a109a0224e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a + 2, b + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056270} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbef1e4-ce60-520e-9c28-990f7d66238b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a + 1, b + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056271} {:answer/id #uuid "3f395b37-6aad-5a65-baba-60a043ec61be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a, b + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056272} {:answer/id #uuid "72236c18-ec22-5d4d-bed9-212581bfdeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a, b + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056273} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7c666d-f03f-5a4c-968c-f04e83032c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a - 1, b + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbef1e4-ce60-520e-9c28-990f7d66238b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a + 1, b + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056271} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df0fc45-813c-5486-99d4-ce01fcdd3aa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "933f2dda-2343-544a-8ef3-2160dd14e9e2" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121220" :question/problem "The point $(a, b)$ lies on the curve $y = x^{2}$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x - 1)^{2} + 2$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b+2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{2}$, we have that ${a}^{2}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+1-1{)}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =b+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b+2)$ must lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3701706-a2ab-527c-a91b-e50ea0d2a4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056276} {:answer/id #uuid "c72b485c-f914-54c6-9862-ca2e77d067be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056277} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5c6281-97f3-5f1f-8de8-37713e91a6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056278} {:answer/id #uuid "ee21d9f5-a100-54a6-b810-c55d4f0b2cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056279} {:answer/id #uuid "38bcd493-ab4a-5d22-b3e3-9133412002a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38bcd493-ab4a-5d22-b3e3-9133412002a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056280} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a6cf1b2-47cd-5307-a39b-ef3cfe5bdbe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bce89a1c-1788-5140-a53a-cfabf6eebe67" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121221" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/141/Source/Images/q-121221.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y = f(x)$. What is the local maximum of $y = f(x - 2) + 1$?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+2,3)=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit upward and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3+1)=(4,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(4,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fa1dedf-3384-504c-b5fe-b7c935c4e2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,3b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242337} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2b9367-f612-5594-9d46-96eb84706463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242338} {:answer/id #uuid "c805b4ba-f771-515a-be67-bf766c50f3df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242339} {:answer/id #uuid "81404138-8b74-582d-9845-2ed3426cfc3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242340} {:answer/id #uuid "44e8a603-073c-50fe-9ac8-b87d0b1dc921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a2b9367-f612-5594-9d46-96eb84706463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242338} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3efcfaf-b884-53eb-acac-188ddab361b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a10acc39-43de-5cce-97e4-fe2c174ea53c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121383" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={2}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={2}^{x-2}+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b+3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={2}^{x}$, we have that ${2}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ Substituting $x=a+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{a+2-2}+3 \\\\  & ={2}^{a}+3 \\\\  & =b+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+2,b+3)$ must lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83ba7556-a7ad-5384-bd3b-a2bfb2a130c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242344} {:answer/id #uuid "0f37a632-fcb0-5eb7-8179-bb757432b129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242345} {:answer/id #uuid "444d2778-897f-5555-9dab-2143abff26f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242346} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a35399-d2a8-5a0d-8719-f2a8c25d53ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242347} {:answer/id #uuid "49f12b0b-5f0f-5dff-8eae-9267bf4e0189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+3,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0a35399-d2a8-5a0d-8719-f2a8c25d53ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242347} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37858ef-144f-5790-8662-8e48d881ca8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92febfde-8323-5fd1-8145-c17f29e2f7b7" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121385" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=|x|.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=|x+3|-2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=|x+3|-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=|x|.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=|x+3|.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-3,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=|x|$, we have that $|a|=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ Substituting $x=a-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =|a-3+3|-2 \\\\  & =|a|-2 \\\\  & =b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-3,b-2)$ must lie on $y=|x+3|-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "953cd5c3-97fa-5808-abf1-c0c3752d07f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242351} {:answer/id #uuid "0130bb54-b057-5ca9-839e-a81783dbb72d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242352} {:answer/id #uuid "a47ef372-6be6-558e-aa9b-b4fef2da5b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242353} {:answer/id #uuid "39fbd5b7-b979-53ef-b7e5-ff2bc4aa874d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242354} {:answer/id #uuid "1a28a9bf-81d2-5aaf-9099-161905647890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0130bb54-b057-5ca9-839e-a81783dbb72d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242352} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd6687de-2238-5f37-9e6f-0763d20353bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5f1d3b8-1a03-52fb-a558-210e8579cf5e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121407" :question/problem "The point  $(-3,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-3,4)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3+1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-(4))=(-2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-2,-4+2)=(-2,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-3,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-3+1)=4\\,⟹\\,f(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-2)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8d5f9a-e300-55ce-b35a-88a9c86329ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242358} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff28aad-c29c-515c-afb4-4e1238ca2bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242359} {:answer/id #uuid "0eeb552e-db1d-5d57-b573-db3e004a76cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242360} {:answer/id #uuid "22a1fc25-0f47-5da2-88fe-7848ed0cfa4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242361} {:answer/id #uuid "3a080da0-b380-5a19-9121-b2d59105962c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a080da0-b380-5a19-9121-b2d59105962c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb8f0439-1450-54f7-b298-b79bbb50d05a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54460c74-a647-5bf9-b4a5-f9969098c52c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121414" :question/problem "The point  $(4,8)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(4,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+3:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8)=(4,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(2))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,-2+3)=(4,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n4f(4)=8\\,⟹\\,f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3e38f7-870d-5c43-b308-779f761d21ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242365} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdaa654-e1cb-5608-bd8a-9b8eebb79e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242366} {:answer/id #uuid "499b9f48-7c20-5f5d-95f0-7450b1e2ede8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242367} {:answer/id #uuid "cd83fa09-2618-55b4-abc6-34d8e3d3d4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242368} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc867c5-1527-58a5-9aaf-23cc044c851f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499b9f48-7c20-5f5d-95f0-7450b1e2ede8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242367} :answer-field/id #uuid "eec2f6d6-894c-565c-8778-6ba3d564b96d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "670d2002-d2ca-56f3-b34d-cba198a70401" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121418" :question/problem "The point  $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3\\cdot 3)=(2,9).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,9)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(9))=(2,-9).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-9)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-9+5)=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(2)=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=9.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+5 \\\\  & =-(9)+5 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b54472c9-257d-519b-a422-d69638e17361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2, 8)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057410} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d9da6b-20f5-5dff-ae07-a9321158ea56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, 8)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057411} {:answer/id #uuid "07d7cb99-1e6a-56ea-90b4-8cb53d58b326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057412} {:answer/id #uuid "13f30848-11d3-57b5-a34a-9e22f244e5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057413} {:answer/id #uuid "2591f00a-e3b1-5ddf-bd02-e878d6fae73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057414} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbd0137-7466-5492-854b-a7318f6f0341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242370} {:answer/id #uuid "3f110463-1eba-550f-af19-ac4156f681f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2591f00a-e3b1-5ddf-bd02-e878d6fae73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057414} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fc38c47-f954-5d7d-9c9a-0e0fe3163b63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c0bb866-f5d6-5c26-9148-409419fa5279" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-186866" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/141/Source/Images/q-186866.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y = f(x)$. What is the global minimum of $y =-2f(x + 1)$?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f53e0abc-1eef-5a15-b22e-fddd77bdf41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242374} {:answer/id #uuid "a37d56e3-e043-5def-ad13-7aa9ca5c8b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242375} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1c7863-f5a1-5b43-b8a5-2b3089fc0aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242376} {:answer/id #uuid "182ddc9d-315c-5e41-b8af-a8c7ad6a8c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242377} {:answer/id #uuid "32119ca0-32e7-5b86-8475-0881f2ce12b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f53e0abc-1eef-5a15-b22e-fddd77bdf41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242374} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5c6c5c-44d3-5c10-8f9d-791c7d973deb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fbc72ae-1559-5655-8419-8ec96a874c40" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-211288" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831127/assets/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+5,-4)=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(3,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ is $(3,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9309cc78-3269-5a16-94cf-ecd12d4c78c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242381} {:answer/id #uuid "133ca763-df80-5101-b301-dfb6ebc1ac45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242382} {:answer/id #uuid "1d93006c-a8c9-5ec2-98c3-0d5dff192810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242383} {:answer/id #uuid "09625741-65f2-5df7-b273-6dd5704cef21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242384} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d4a6fe-6f58-5aa5-b259-a7015844b6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9309cc78-3269-5a16-94cf-ecd12d4c78c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242381} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f02a04b-2be1-53ab-bbb4-250a2c621376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f4525c5-5e4e-5b55-8b15-c9fe5125a6ae" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-211303" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831127/assets/ff2956aa2885a0abddd614a11e632d3dfd1114391a9d7e27d790746c159e7f79.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis,\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-1),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-3f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3\\cdot (-1))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-1\\cdot (-3))=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3-1)=(4,2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1$ is $(4,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2206ebab-a00d-5166-9813-50c7bbb5a8f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242388} {:answer/id #uuid "3a241e67-b5fb-5239-9bed-c11ce05a60f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242389} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8b085c-adbe-5ddf-aa9e-307d6a3e5152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242390} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe19725-c4d0-5d75-906f-685d14540edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242391} {:answer/id #uuid "578ed652-b7a7-50fc-b2a7-aec34ebfe02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a241e67-b5fb-5239-9bed-c11ce05a60f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242389} :answer-field/id #uuid "b84059e6-392d-5ff7-b216-2452474ee3bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb74a779-9886-5633-be8a-07053e04402f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-211466" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831127/assets/2d046da9a89fe09f09dbd5e0658422ab8ccee8db680a2955ebe1ff0f361b5c35.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of \n$y=-4f(3x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-4f(3x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-4f(3x).$ So, we need to find the image of $(3,1),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-4f(3x),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(3,1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3,1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,4\\cdot 1)=(1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 4)=(1,-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-4f(3x)$ is $(1,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b641ac-d16b-57ce-a4d0-3f44c7483db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058058} {:answer/id #uuid "b445612f-0f49-53f2-80eb-e56a1240a545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058059} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d5fb6a-847c-5cc0-b785-a0ece94dd259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058060} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb834e2-a78e-588c-9eac-c20f4d3e4752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058061} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9357d8-be0e-545e-9fb3-30854a64a991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b445612f-0f49-53f2-80eb-e56a1240a545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058059} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6ce53cd-345c-5bf6-ab15-b3f7219d94ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4d7f8b8-ba0a-5db0-954f-1969ddfefa81" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-26398" :question/problem "The point $(1, 4)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = f(x - 1) + 1$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of \n$y=f(x-1).$  After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1+1,4)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we shift the result vertically by $1$  unit up and obtain the graph of\n$y=f(x-1)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,4+1)=(2,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x-1)+1.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2-1)+1 \\\\  & =f(1)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c99c320f-2a35-5a04-ad64-030678d28fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242395} {:answer/id #uuid "018bc939-0d73-5794-b54c-fe49e335c40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242396} {:answer/id #uuid "401014e4-b5bb-53dd-984e-927695931a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242397} {:answer/id #uuid "4f511992-425d-5e2c-9118-8629084264e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242398} {:answer/id #uuid "cbade78c-41b7-5009-a7f2-dea33ed72507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f511992-425d-5e2c-9118-8629084264e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242398} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7755b91-5d25-5b7f-baeb-83497373fe06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a300bbe-a008-544f-8314-4d9f7fffaeff" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-26408" :question/problem "The point $(-1,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,3-1)=(-1,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2))=(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,-2+2)=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-1)+1=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb90d16-e7d0-5e52-a9fd-df72d647e0c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242402} {:answer/id #uuid "ad368c5b-0cb2-529b-9e46-903b3993c33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242403} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad6f9c9-32b7-5ae8-beed-184a772b2483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242404} {:answer/id #uuid "153c2ce8-7eb9-5641-b916-f32801aad129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242405} {:answer/id #uuid "2984184b-7a52-589b-81d1-99ec20577215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad368c5b-0cb2-529b-9e46-903b3993c33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242403} :answer-field/id #uuid "71cc3638-abd0-5b43-9039-38f6aabc33fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6d26fbc-87de-5a39-ae14-d0903e431edc" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-26594" :question/problem "The point  $(3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1))=(3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1-1)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(3)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bbcd33a-e392-52d2-bc13-c126c751c9f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242409} {:answer/id #uuid "3aedfbff-de2f-5a2b-a4d4-d1c3278fd732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242410} {:answer/id #uuid "b158e99c-91f5-504e-8d31-837ded092ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242411} {:answer/id #uuid "4779b50b-0ef2-5bc2-865a-ce8fcf519744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242412} {:answer/id #uuid "0f7aa2b2-7116-59cc-a55d-6a6257726630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aedfbff-de2f-5a2b-a4d4-d1c3278fd732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242410} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82234cd-b2c2-5bfe-9450-e9c9beee279c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a08d6bcc-6c8d-59c1-9671-aa0f95bb9926" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-26596" :question/problem "The point  $(3,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(3,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,2+2)=(3,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(4))=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-4+2)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-2=2\\,⟹\\,f(3)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"}]}
